will carefully examine the relationship between (1)-(3) and (4)-(5) and conclude that (3) is a more tenable view than (1). In section 1 I will specify (1)-(3) about how to respond to peer disagreement. In section 2, I will introduce (4)-(5) about the relationship between rationality and evidence and examine their relationship with (1)-(3). It leads to the conclusion that (2) can be dismissed. In section 3 I will argue that (5) on the relationship between evidence and rationality is more acceptable than (4). In section 4 I will compare two combinations of (1), (3) and (5) and argue that the combination of (3) and (5) is more tenable or at least equally plausible."}, {"id": "https://openalex.org/W2336005475", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199226078.003.0007", "title": "Peer Disagreement and Higher‐Order Evidence", "publication_year": 2010, "type": "book-chapter", "cited_by_count": 581, "open_access": {"is_oa": true, "oa_status": "green", "oa_url": "https://philpapers.org/archive/KELPDA.pdf", "any_repository_has_fulltext": true}, "primary_location": {"id": "doi:10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199226078.003.0007", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199226078.003.0007", "is_oa": false, "landing_page_url": "https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199226078.003.0007", "pdf_url": null, "source": {"id": "https://openalex.org/S4306463708", "display_name": "Oxford University Press eBooks", "issn_l": null, "issn": null, "is_oa": false, "is_in_doaj": false, "is_core": false, "listed_in": [], "host_organization": "https://openalex.org/P4310311648", "host_organization_name": "Oxford University Press", "host_organization_lineage": ["https://openalex.org/P4310311648", "https://openalex.org/P4310311647"], "host_organization_lineage_names": ["Oxford University Press", "University of Oxford"], "type": "ebook platform"}, "license": null, "license_id": null, "version": "publishedVersion", "is_accepted": true, "is_published": true, "raw_source_name": "Disagreement", "raw_type": "book-chapter"}, "abstract": "This chapter distinguishes and assesses a set of views about the rational response to peer disagreement. It raises a series of objections to views according to which one should assign ‘equal weight’ to one's own view and the views of those with which one disagrees. It concludes by defending ‘The Total Evidence View’, according to which what one is justified in believing in cases of peer disagreement depends upon what is supported by one's total evidence."}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "ad42be2dbd21a696a3b479fd9c8fd6cbc51daad1fef5fbf313df549c24915525", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "judgment_and_consensus", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.5840/eps201956231", "doi:10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199226078.003.0007", "openalex:W2945461406", "openalex:W2336005475"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.5840/eps201956231", "https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199226078.003.0007"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}
